🚀 Interview Reflection: CI/CD Strategy for On-Prem Kubernetes

In a recent interview, I went through some real-life DevOps questions;
🔹 How do you manage complete CI/CD in your environments?
 🔹 Given only three phases — Deploy, Monitor, Review — how would you approach a secure CI/CD pipeline?
 🔹 What is your secrets management strategy?
 
I was also asked to design a secure, scalable, and highly available CI/CD pipeline for AWS Kubernetes setup, based on a real-world architecture:
Architecture Flow:
Frontend (React) → API → Queue (Kafka) → Worker → Database
The interviewer posed several thought-provoking questions:

 🔹 Which non-cloud/unmanaged tools would you use for deploying this architecture and how?
 🔹 How would you enhance scalability, security, and availability of the deployments?
🔹 Suppose we are using 'mysql' for our database load, which is so critical and no downtime required ever as well as want high performance. Will this database serve the purpose as we donot want to use any cloud managed service. if yes, how?
 🔹 What would be your monitoring approach for this setup?
These questions pushed me to think deeply about GitOps, secrets management with Vault, Kafka scaling with Strimzi, and observability using Prometheus, Grafana, Loki, and Jaeger — all within a self-hosted ecosystem.
💡 It was a great reminder that cloud-native principles can be applied even in non-cloud environments, and that tooling choices play a critical role in building resilient systems.
